# Fix the Actual Bug, Not the Surrounding Code

## The Anti-Pattern

When something doesn't work, the lazy fix is to reshape whatever is easiest to change — even if that thing was already correct. Stop and ask: **what is actually wrong here?**

Seeing "the skip link queries `main` and this page doesn't have `main`" and jumping to "change the page to use `main`" is backwards. The page's DOM is the source of truth for what the content *is*. The JS selector is the consumer — it should adapt, not the other way around.

Seeing a `<button aria-hidden="true">` hiding content from screen readers and replacing the `<button>` with a `<div>` is fixing the symptom by introducing a new problem. The button was semantically correct. `aria-hidden="true"` was the bug. Remove the attribute, keep the element.

## The Rule

**Always find the minimal correct fix.** Before making a change, ask:

1. **Which side is wrong?** When two things don't match, identify which one is the source of truth. DOM structure that describes what content *is* almost always wins over JS that consumes it.
2. **Am I fixing the bug, or just the symptom?** If the fix involves changing something that was already correct to accommodate something that's broken, flip it around.
3. **What's the smallest change that solves this?** A targeted attribute removal or selector update beats a structural refactor. Don't reach for a bigger hammer when a screwdriver works.

## Examples

```typescript
// ❌ LAZY — changing a page's <section> to <main> because a skip link queries `main`
// The page used <section> intentionally; the skip link selector is the bug.

// ✅ RIGHT — fix the selector to match whatever landmark the page uses
const target = document.querySelector('main, section, [role="main"]');
```

```html
<!-- ❌ LAZY — replacing a <button> with a <div> to remove aria-hidden -->
<!-- The button was correct; aria-hidden="true" was the only problem. -->
<div class="tab-inner" @click="${this._handleClick}">...</div>

<!-- ✅ RIGHT — remove the bad attribute, keep the correct element -->
<button tabindex="-1" @click="${this._handleClick}">...</button>
```
